      "body": "The S&P 500 gained 0.42% to close at 7,575.39 and the Nasdaq-100 rose roughly 0.85%, powered by Nvidia (+4.0%) and Meta (+6.0% after a Bank of America note flagged lower-than-expected internal AI computing costs), while South Korean memory-chip maker SK Hynix soared 13% in its $26.5 billion U.S. listing debut.",
      "explain": "Meta's rally on a cost memo rather than a revenue beat matters because AI capex bulls and bears are really debating the same number: how much compute a dollar of spending buys, and cheaper-than-expected inference costs directly raise the operating margin the market prices into every AI-linked stock, not just Meta's. SK Hynix's 13% pop is the same trade from the supply side — it is one of two dominant producers of the high-bandwidth memory chips that feed Nvidia's AI accelerators, so a hot U.S. listing signals investors betting memory demand from AI clusters stays structurally elevated rather than fading. Today's Deep Dive walks through why memory chips, not just GPUs, are the actual bottleneck in this cycle."
