      "body": "WTI crude fell about 1.0% to roughly $71.90 and Brent slipped toward $75.50, extending Thursday's more-than-2% drop, after President Trump said Iran had reached out about restarting negotiations — even though Brent remains on pace for a roughly 4-5% gain on the week given how far the war-risk premium had spiked earlier.",
      "explain": "This is the same repricing mechanism from Wednesday's Iran-strike spike running in reverse: traders aren't pricing that the conflict ended, they're pricing that the specific tail scenario — a closed Strait of Hormuz or seized tankers — is now less likely given an active diplomatic channel, so the premium bleeds out even before any deal is signed. The move is a reminder that oil's risk premium tracks the probability of disruption, which can swing on a single reported phone call, while the fundamental supply-demand price underneath moves far more slowly."
